/// # The clos2 Plugin {#clos2}
///
/// @see mim::plug::clos2
///
/// [TOC]
///
/// This plugin performs *Typed Closure Conversion*.
///
/// ## Dependencies
///
plugin core;
import compile;
///
/// ## Operations
///
/// ## Phases

/// ### Phase I
axm hoist_up: compile.Phase;
/// This phase is performing something similar to the abstract closure conversion from the 2018 paper.
/// MimIR does not provide the functionality needed to do it exactly, thus I'm simply using delayed partial application
/// together with explicit annotation to essentially archive the same.
///
/// Every Lambda that fulfils some criteria will be closure converted.
///
/// The transformation is essentially
///
/// TODO: put the transformation as tex formula here
///
/// In the target world this "closure" is then marked as such using the following wrapper which's normalizer always rejects
priv axm anno.(closure): {T:*} → T → T, normalize_anno;

/// ### Phase II
axm lam_lift_type: compile.Phase;
/// This phase lambda lifts variables from the environment which appear in the type of the actual lambda into the domain of that lambda.
/// Doing so enforces the following restriction in the target world.
///
/// > The type of a Lambda must not have any free variables.

/// ### Phase III
axm clos_conv: compile.Phase;
/// In the source world we expect the restriction from above to be fulfilled.
/// It enables the usage of existentials to eliminate the abstract closures.
